A||N||11||FieroJoe||Uneven brake pad wear??||||4||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||05-25-2001||07:42 PM||||Recently my car has been pulling quite a bit to the left when I brake normally.  If I am driving in a straight line, and press the pedal slightly, the car dances to the left a little bit and then straightens itself out.  It is not like it keeps on going left.<P>At first I thought maybe there was some air in the calipers, so I bled the brakes.  They were all air free.  I also noticed that on all 4 of my calipers, the outer pads were worn almost 100% more than the inner.  Is this normal for floating caliper type brakes? <P>I dont know if it would have to do with my car pulling while braking, but why the uneven wear?  It seems to me that the pad right next to the piston would get to most force, and thus wear the most, instead of the other way around.<P>Is this uneven wear a possible cause for my pulling while braking?  Just seems very odd to me...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||4
Z||000001||thomas_l||05-25-2001||07:49 PM||||Could be one of the right calipers is sticking. The left side hits first causing the pull, then the right side grabs. Check to see that the rears are adjusting in (I would assume they are since you didn't say low pedal. Try greasing the slides first to make sure the pressure is going to both pads.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Her86GT||05-25-2001||08:02 PM||||I would raise the frt wheels and see if they spin freely with brakes released.  If one does not find out why-possible seized caliper.  If they both spin freely have one person slowly apply the brake with engine off and spin both frt wheels by hand and see if the brakes apply first on one before the other.  Likely this is what you will find. Then have the person press hard on the brake pedal and release brake and see if both release at the same time. If one stays on for a bit longer than releases to spin freely replace that flex line. If one stays on longer and stays tight replace that caliper.  This is how I diag a bad flex line on my car.  My symptoms were when I applied the brake the car would pull hard to left and than straighten.  This was due to collapsed flex line.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||05-25-2001||08:03 PM||||Most definatly grease the pins. Chances are yours are frozen and dragging the outer pad<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||sjp777||05-25-2001||08:03 PM||||Hanging caliper. I replaced my front pads about a year back and when I did it I noticed the pass side pads were way more worn than the driver's. About 3 months after I did the pads, pass side caliper locked up entirely and had to be replaced<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||05-26-2001||05:34 PM||||I will have to go and grease those slide bolts up next time that I get a chance to.  I find this odd that all 4 outer pads are worn so much more than the inner, but I guess it is possible that all 4 calipers arent moving correctly.<P>All 4 wheels spin freely, there isnt any pad drag (unless they've already worn so much since they were new that they wouldnt drag anymore...)<P>About the collapsed flex line, is there any way to tell visually if it needs to be replaced?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-26-2001||05:39 PM||||Keep in mind that the outer pad and inner pad aren't the same thickness to start with. Outers are typically half the thickness of inners when new.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||05-26-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Keep in mind that the outer pad and inner pad aren't the same thickness to start with. Outers are typically half the thickness of inners when new.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ah hah! I see.  Any particular reason for them to be different?||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||05-27-2001||02:47 AM||||The puck is pushing the hardest on the inner pad since there is a lot of mechanical(binding) drag in pulling the entire caliper over to get the outer pad to contact the rotor. especially if the slides are dry.<P>Thats why performance brakes have pucks on both sides of the caliper.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||Phil||05-27-2001||07:39 AM||||If it's an 88 all the pads are the same thickness||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-27-2001||01:03 PM||||88,,, yup... those pads should be the same... <P>84-87 inner is thicker than outer. Outer also has more surface than inner. So they end up wearing differantly even when they work perfectly.<P><BR>No-one said what year...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GTZ34||05-27-2001||01:17 PM||||Ogre, his signature says '87.  You are correct sir.  I think Phil was just mentioning the difference in the '88s for those of us following along.||||reg||1
